#!/usr/bin/env ruby

# THE OTHER HALF OF "NOTICE IT AND ACT" — `bin/qa_sweep` finds a bug by
# fuzzing; this finds one by asking GitHub what it already knows. Same
# contract shape, same reason: deterministic, mechanical, an exit code an
# agent (or a loop) reads instead of a transcript it has to parse.
#
# THE GAP THIS CLOSES. A `loop-parity/*` PR is a fix `bin/qa_sweep`'s own
# agent judged self-contained and verified LOCALLY before opening it —
# but "verified locally" and "CI's own fresh build agrees" are two
# different facts, and until this script existed nothing in the ledger
# ever checked whether they still matched. A PR could go red days later —
# a fix that happened to work on one machine's cache, a flake that wasn't,
# a merge with `main` that broke it — and the only thing that noticed was
# a human scrolling GitHub's own PR list. `QualityControl::Bug::Regress`
# already said the right word ("put a bug back when its fix stopped
# holding") and nothing had ever dispatched it — `BugCiWatch`
# (qa/bluebook/quality_control.bluebook) is the process manager that
# turns a `Clearance::Failed` into a `Bug::Regress` on its own,
# automatically, the moment a clearance is recorded for the commit a Bug
# named as its fix.
#
# THE DOMAIN DOES THE CHECKING — this script does not. It used to
# dispatch `Clearance::Start`/`Passed`/`Failed` directly, which was a
# shortcut around two policies the chapter had ALREADY declared for
# exactly this (`ClearOnPass`/`RefuseOnFail`, `quality_control.bluebook`'s
# own foot), leaving them dead code forever. What this script does now:
# `Clearance.Start`, to put the commit on the record — still a direct
# dispatch, because starting a run is this script's own decision, not an
# external fact — and then `Clearance.CI.Run`, the ASK the chapter's own
# `CI` port declares (`quality_control.hecksagon`). `GithubChecks`
# (`qa/adapters/github_checks.rb`) is what actually shells to `gh` from
# there and answers or refuses; `ClearOnPass`/`RefuseOnFail` are what turn
# that into `Clearance::Passed`/`Failed`, for real, the same way
# `Ticket.Raise` already lets `FileWhenSubmitted` drive `IssueTracker`
# rather than filing anything by hand.
#
# SECOND GAP CLOSED HERE: WHICH PRs, AND WHICH COMMITS, ARE EVEN WORTH
# ASKING ABOUT. Both used to be plain Ruby, outside the ledger. Neither
# is any more — see "PR DISCOVERY" and "WHICH COMMITS ARE WORTH ASKING
# THE LEDGER ABOUT" below for what changed and why, with the real
# evidence each change was checked against.
#
#   bin/qa_pr_check
#
# WHAT THIS REUSES RATHER THAN REINVENTS: the `gh` CLI, already this
# repository's own way of talking to GitHub (`.claude/skills/hecks_qa/
# SKILL.md`'s own `gh pr list`/`gh pr view` examples) — nothing here opens
# a second HTTP client or re-derives a token. `gh pr checks <n> --json`
# is still what THIS script uses to decide whether a PR is even worth
# asking the ledger about yet (see "PENDING STAYS OUTSIDE THE PORT"
# below); `GithubChecks` reaches `gh` on its own, by commit, for the
# verdict itself.
#
# PENDING STAYS OUTSIDE THE PORT, ON PURPOSE. `Clearance`'s own two
# endings are `Passed`/`Failed` — final, and minted once per commit
# forever (see `Clearance`'s own header). There is no "ask me again
# later" a `refuses` could mean without lying: refusing "checks are still
# running" would read exactly like refusing "checks failed," and
# `BugCiWatch` cannot tell those apart. So this script still checks
# whether a PR's checks have finished BEFORE it ever starts a `Clearance`
# for that commit — the one decision that has to happen out here, because
# the port's own vocabulary has nowhere to put it.
#
# PR DISCOVERY — BROADENED, AND CHECKED AGAINST REAL HISTORY BEFORE
# COMMITTING TO IT. This used to be `gh pr list --search "head:loop-
# parity"` alone, which missed any PR `hecks_qa` opened on a differently
# named branch — real, not hypothetical: PR #534 this very session
# ("qa: fix quality_control.world's dropdb guidance...") sat on
# `worktree-agent-a40959be25ea6c419`, invisible to that search, purely
# because of its branch name.
#
# THE OBVIOUS FIX — match on the PR's own TITLE prefix `qa:` instead —
# turned out NOT to be a strict improvement, and this was checked, not
# assumed (`gh pr list --state all --search "in:title qa:"` against
# `head:loop-parity --state all`, both run for real against this
# session's own PR history before writing a line of this script).
# `head:loop-parity`, all-time, includes #533 ("heki: explicit, opt-in
# journal compaction..."), #532 ("BUG#6: saga dispatch..."), #529
# ("rust: NotFound wins..."), and #526 ("rust: entity addressing...") —
# four real `hecks_qa`-originated PRs on `loop-parity/*` branches whose
# TITLES do not start with `qa:` at all. Title-only discovery would have
# made those four invisible the same way branch-only discovery made #534
# invisible. Neither list is a superset of the other, so the fix is the
# UNION of both, not a replacement of one by the other — every open PR is
# fetched once and kept if EITHER its branch starts with `loop-parity/`
# OR its title starts with `qa:`.
#
# ONE CLEARANCE PER COMMIT, MINTED ONCE — the same reading `Clearance`'s
# own header comment gives: "a commit's CI result doesn't change once
# settled... a later commit simply has none of its own." So a commit that
# already has a `Clearance` (green OR red) is never re-asked. The thing
# that changes over time is which COMMIT a PR's branch points at (a push
# mints a new sha, therefore a new, still-unminted `Clearance` to check
# next run), never what an already-recorded commit's own run said.
#
# WHICH COMMITS ARE WORTH ASKING THE LEDGER ABOUT — MOVED INTO THE
# LEDGER. This used to be raw Ruby here: load every `Clearance`, walk the
# list by hand, skip a commit if one already named it. That was "has this
# already been checked" logic living OUTSIDE the one chapter that already
# has an opinion about every other question like it — so it is gone, in
# favour of `QualityControl::Bug::AwaitingClearance`
# (qa/bluebook/quality_control.bluebook), a real query the chapter itself
# declares: bugs with a commit recorded that CI has not yet given a
# verdict on. This script's own job shrinks to two things neither
# `AwaitingClearance` nor `Clearance` could know on their own: which PRs
# GitHub currently has open, and whether THIS run's checks have actually
# finished — everything past that is the ledger's own answer, read back,
# not re-derived.
#
# EXIT CODES — the only contract an agent (or a loop) needs to read:
#   0  nothing to act on — no open `qa:`/`loop-parity/*` PRs at all, or
#      every one of them already has a recorded Clearance (or its checks
#      are still pending, not yet a verdict) and nothing new was
#      dispatched.
#   1  an operational error — `gh` was not reachable at all, a Bug's own
#      recorded commit does not look like a sha, or the ledger itself
#      would not boot. Fix the actual problem; there is nothing here for
#      an agent to pick up as QA work.
#   2  FOUND A NEWLY-RED PR — a `Clearance::Failed` was dispatched this
#      run for a commit a `Bug` in this ledger claims to have fixed,
#      which means `BugCiWatch` just fired `Bug.Regress` for real. Full
#      detail (which Bug, which PR, which commit, which checks failed) is
#      on stdout above the exit. An agent picks up the investigation from
#      there — same judgment SKILL.md's own "on a surprising check"
#      section already describes for a sweep's own find: self-contained
#      fix with a regression test and a draft PR, or leave the (now
#      re-opened) Bug for something bigger. Never re-verify or re-close
#      this script's own way; that is exactly the judgment step it does
#      not have.
#
# WHAT THIS SCRIPT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: decide whether a red run
# means the fix was wrong, judge which failing check matters, write a new
# regression test, open a second PR, decide which commits still owe CI an
# answer, or compute a pass/fail verdict of its own — all three of those
# last used to happen here and now happen behind the port or inside the
# chapter's own query instead. It records what the ledger says once
# asked and stops — the process manager already turned a red answer into
# a `Bug.Regress` by the time this script's own exit code says so;
# anything past that is the same investigation `bin/qa_sweep`'s own "on a
# surprising check" judgment already covers, reused rather than restated
# here.

$LOAD_PATH.unshift File.expand_path("../lib", __dir__)

require "hecks"
# ADR 0033 — see bin/run's own identical require: the era/lineage
# subsystem no longer loads with core, and this ledger's `persisted_by
# "Heki"` binding needs it explicitly.
require "hecks/ports/persistence/plugins/era"
require "json"
require "open3"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
# THE SHARED "START IT" STEP — `qa/adapters/github_ci_webhook.rb`'s own
# push trigger needs the identical guarded-start this script's pull
# trigger does, so it lives once, in `Hecks::QA::ClearanceRecorder`. See
# that file's own header for why `Start` is the part actually shared and
# `Passed`/`Failed` is not.
require_relative "../qa/adapters/clearance_recorder"

DOMAIN_DIR = File.join(ROOT, "qa/bluebook")

EXIT_OK        = 0
EXIT_ERROR     = 1
EXIT_NEWLY_RED = 2

USAGE = "usage: bin/qa_pr_check".freeze

SHA_PATTERN = /\A[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}\z/

# A PR COUNTS AS `hecks_qa`'S OWN if EITHER its branch is a `loop-parity/*`
# fix or its title is one of this practice's own reports — see this
# file's own header, "PR DISCOVERY", for the real history this union was
# checked against.
BRANCH_PREFIX = "loop-parity/".freeze
TITLE_PREFIX  = "qa:".freeze

unless ARGV.empty?
  if %w[-h --help].include?(ARGV.first)
    puts USAGE
    exit EXIT_OK
  end
  abort "#{USAGE}\nthis script takes no arguments — it checks every open qa:/loop-parity/* PR, every time"
end

# ── talk to gh, once per call, never guessing at its own exit codes ────
#
# `--json` ALREADY MEANS "I am reading this programmatically" — gh's own
# special exit codes (8 for "checks pending", among others) are reserved
# for its interactive/watch modes and do not fire once `--json` is given;
# confirmed live rather than assumed (`gh pr checks <n> --json ...` exits
# 0 whether every check passed or several failed). So this reads the
# `bucket` field itself for the real verdict and treats gh's own exit
# code as answering only one question: did the CALL succeed at all.
def gh(*)
  Open3.capture3("gh", *)
end

# ── boot the ledger for real ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE REAL `CI` PORT COMES BOUND — `qa/adapters/github_checks.{adapter,
# rb}` is what `Folder#load_project` picks up during this boot (see that
# file's own header for why no line here has to name it), binding
# `GithubChecks` to the port `quality_control.hecksagon` declares. Boot
# this domain any other way (`spec/quality_control_spec.rb`'s own inline
# hecksagon, `bin/model_check`'s static-only load) and the port stays
# exactly as dormant as `IssueTracker`'s still is — real binding is a
# property of THIS boot, not of the chapter.

runtime = begin
  Hecks.boot(DOMAIN_DIR)
rescue StandardError => e
  abort "the QualityControl ledger did not boot — fix the ledger itself before checking PRs against it " \
        "(#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
end

def q(runtime, name, **args) = runtime.query("QualityControl::#{name}", **args)

# ── every open qa:/loop-parity/* PR, from gh's own mouth ────────────────
#
# ONE UNFILTERED CALL, THEN A CLIENT-SIDE UNION — rather than two
# `--search` calls glued together, or a single `--search` expression
# leaning on `gh`'s own OR syntax. Every open PR in this repository is a
# small, cheap list (`gh pr list --state open` with no search at all),
# and filtering it here in the open — `branch?(pr) || title?(pr)`, read
# top to bottom — is what makes the union this file's header describes
# something a reader can actually verify against the code, rather than
# trusting a search-string dialect neither `gh` nor GitHub's own search
# documents in the same place twice.

list_out, list_err, list_status = gh("pr", "list", "--state", "open", "--limit", "200",
                                     "--json", "number,headRefName,headRefOid,url,title")

unless list_status.success?
  abort "gh pr list could not reach GitHub — #{list_err.strip.empty? ? list_out.strip : list_err.strip}"
end

def qa_branch?(pull_request) = pull_request[:headRefName].to_s.start_with?(BRANCH_PREFIX)
def qa_title?(pull_request)  = pull_request[:title].to_s.start_with?(TITLE_PREFIX)

prs = JSON.parse(list_out, symbolize_names: true).select { |pr| qa_branch?(pr) || qa_title?(pr) }

if prs.empty?
  puts "no open qa:/loop-parity/* PRs — nothing to check"
  exit EXIT_OK
end

bugs     = q(runtime, "Bug.All")
awaiting = q(runtime, "Bug.AwaitingClearance")

# `sha.start_with?(commit) || commit.start_with?(sha)` — A BUG'S OWN
# `commit` MAY BE SHORT. Nothing in this ledger requires a full 40-char
# sha (`CommitRef`'s own pattern accepts 7-40), and `gh`'s own
# `headRefOid` is always full, so an exact `==` would silently miss every
# bug fixed with a short sha — the common case, since that is what `git
# rev-parse --short HEAD` and most people typing a sha by hand both give.
# `field(row, :commit, :value)`, NOT `row.dig(:commit, :value)` — a query
# row's own field values are `Hecks::Runtime::Value` objects, which answer
# `[]` (the same reader the facade and every spec in this repository
# already use) but not `Hash#dig`. Confirmed live, not assumed: `.dig`
# raises `TypeError: Hecks::Runtime::Value does not have #dig method` the
# first time this script ever ran against a real boot.
def field(row, *path)
  path.reduce(row) { |held, key| held.nil? ? nil : held[key] }
end

# SHARED BY BOTH `bugs` (every bug ever logged) AND `awaiting` (the
# ledger's own opinion on which of those still owe CI an answer) — the
# same short/long-sha reconciliation either list needs, run against
# whichever rows the caller hands it.
def bug_for_commit(rows, sha)
  rows.find do |bug|
    commit = field(bug, :commit, :value)
    next false if commit.to_s.empty?

    sha.start_with?(commit) || commit.start_with?(sha)
  end
end

# COSMETIC ONLY — read for the human-readable "already cleared (...)"
# message, never for the worth-checking DECISION itself. That decision is
# `bug_for_commit(awaiting, sha)` below, which is the ledger's own
# `Bug.AwaitingClearance` answer, not a second, hand-rolled reading of
# `Clearance.All`.
def clearance_for_commit(runtime, sha)
  q(runtime, "Clearance.All").find { |row| field(row, :commit, :value) == sha }
end

# ONE PR, START TO FINISH — pulled out of the loop below so the loop
# itself reads as "for every open PR, check it" rather than burying that
# shape under every early-exit this one PR's own checking needs. Appends
# to `newly_red`/`operational_errors` in place rather than returning a
# verdict to fold in, the same reporting shape `bin/qa_sweep`'s own
# per-seed loop uses.
def check_one_pr(pull_request, runtime, bugs, awaiting, newly_red, operational_errors)
  number = pull_request[:number]
  sha    = pull_request[:headRefOid].to_s
  label  = "PR ##{number} (#{pull_request[:headRefName]})"

  unless sha.match?(SHA_PATTERN)
    operational_errors << "#{label}: head commit #{sha.inspect} does not look like a sha — skipping"
    return
  end

  bug = bug_for_commit(bugs, sha)
  unless bug
    puts "#{label}, #{sha[0, 7]}: no Bug in the ledger names this commit as its fix — skipping"
    return
  end

  bug_ref = field(bug, :reference, :value)

  # THE DOMAIN'S OWN ANSWER, READ BACK — NOT RE-DERIVED. A commit absent
  # from `Bug.AwaitingClearance` is one the chapter itself already
  # considers settled (see that query's own header on the two
  # `none_in_state` clauses this means). `clearance_for_commit` below is
  # only reached to make the message readable; it decides nothing.
  unless bug_for_commit(awaiting, sha)
    existing = clearance_for_commit(runtime, sha)
    status   = existing ? existing[:status] : "already cleared"
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: already cleared (#{status}) — settled once, not re-checked"
    return
  end

  # THE ONE PLACE THIS SCRIPT STILL LOOKS AT `gh` ITSELF — deciding
  # whether it is even worth asking the ledger yet. See this file's own
  # header, "PENDING STAYS OUTSIDE THE PORT."
  checks_out, checks_err, checks_status = gh("pr", "checks", number.to_s,
                                             "--json", "name,state,bucket,workflow")
  unless checks_status.success?
    operational_errors << "#{label}: gh pr checks failed — #{checks_err.strip.empty? ? checks_out.strip : checks_err.strip}"
    return
  end

  checks = JSON.parse(checks_out, symbolize_names: true)
  if checks.empty?
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: gh reports no checks at all yet — skipping this run"
    return
  end

  if checks.any? { |c| c[:bucket] == "pending" }
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: checks still running — skipping this run"
    return
  end

  ask_the_ledger(sha, label, bug_ref, number, runtime, newly_red)
end

# THE ASK, AND THE VERDICT READ BACK — `Clearance.Start` still happens
# here (starting a run is this script's own decision, and it is the one
# step `qa/adapters/github_ci_webhook.rb`'s own push trigger needs
# guarded the identical way — see `ClearanceRecorder.ensure_started`'s
# own header for why that step, and only that step, is shared). Everything
# past it is `Clearance.CI.Run` — the `CI` port's own `asks "Run"` — and
# the two policies the chapter already declared. `GithubChecks` answers
# or refuses from what `gh` says about THIS commit; `ClearOnPass`/
# `RefuseOnFail` are what turn that into `Clearance::Passed`/`Failed`,
# synchronously, before `runtime.dispatch` returns — so `Clearance.find`
# right after already sees the settled record. THIS is the one place in
# this script that must keep going through the port rather than settling
# directly the way the webhook does: `GithubChecks` is the only thing
# that actually knows how to turn raw `check-runs` JSON into green or
# red, and routing around it here would leave `ClearOnPass`/
# `RefuseOnFail` permanently undispatched — exactly the dead-policy trap
# this file's own header already warns against reintroducing.
def ask_the_ledger(sha, label, bug_ref, number, runtime, newly_red)
  Hecks::QA::ClearanceRecorder.ensure_started(sha)
  runtime.dispatch("QualityControl::Clearance.CI.Run", commit: sha)

  cleared = QualityControl::Clearance.find(sha)
  summary = cleared.summary.to_h[:value]

  if cleared.status == "green"
    puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: CLEARED — #{summary}"
    return
  end

  puts "#{label}, #{bug_ref}, #{sha[0, 7]}: RED — #{summary}"
  newly_red << { pr: number, bug: bug_ref, commit: sha, refusal: summary }
end

newly_red = []
operational_errors = []

prs.each { |pull_request| check_one_pr(pull_request, runtime, bugs, awaiting, newly_red, operational_errors) }

# ── report and exit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

unless operational_errors.empty?
  puts
  puts "operational error(s):"
  operational_errors.each { |message| puts "  #{message}" }
end

if newly_red.any?
  puts
  puts "=" * 72
  puts "FOUND #{newly_red.length} NEWLY-RED PR(S) — BugCiWatch already dispatched Bug.Regress for each."
  puts "An agent picks up the investigation from here: the same judgment"
  puts "SKILL.md's own \"on a surprising check\" section describes for a sweep's"
  puts "own find — self-contained fix with a regression test and a draft PR,"
  puts "or leave the bug for something bigger. Never re-verify from this script."
  puts "=" * 72
  newly_red.each do |finding|
    puts
    puts "bug:     #{finding[:bug]}"
    puts "pr:      ##{finding[:pr]}"
    puts "commit:  #{finding[:commit]}"
    puts "refusal: #{finding[:refusal]}"
  end
  exit EXIT_NEWLY_RED
end

exit EXIT_ERROR if operational_errors.any?

puts
puts "clean — every open qa:/loop-parity/* PR is either already cleared, still running, or unmatched to a Bug."
exit EXIT_OK
